  "tax_note": "Adult-use (non-medical) purchases carry a 10% excise tax plus state and local sales tax; medical purchases pay only regular sales tax (Ohio Department of Taxation; R.C. 3796.40).",
  "summary": "Ohio began non-medical sales on August 6, 2024, at existing medical dispensaries, and the adult-use side has quickly become the bigger business. In the 52 weeks to December 27, 2025, dispensaries sold $1.07 billion, of which $829.4 million was adult-use. By October 24, 2026, cumulative non-medical sales had passed $2 billion. Adult-use demand keeps rising as more dual-use dispensaries open, while medical sales are falling as more patients buy at adult-use counters. Ohio publishes running totals rather than monthly figures, so period comparisons here use 4- and 5-week windows.",
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much cannabis does Ohio sell?",
      "a": "In the four weeks ending 2026-10-24, Ohio dispensaries sold $108.5 million, including $94.5 million non-medical, based on DCC cumulative totals."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does Ohio publish monthly cannabis sales?",
      "a": "No. The Division of Cannabis Control publishes weekly running totals since launch. Period sales here are the differences between those totals."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much cannabis did Ohio sell in 2025?",
      "a": "About $1.07 billion in the 52 weeks ending December 27, 2025: $829.4 million adult-use and $236.0 million medical."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the cannabis tax in Ohio?",
      "a": "Adult-use purchases carry a 10% excise tax plus state and local sales tax. Medical purchases pay only sales tax."
    }
  ],
